Wire algebra type conflicts into errors

build_structures already collects per-rule TypeConflicts during the
populate/close loop, but build.rs threw the result away. Capture the
errors and chain them into the same merge that picks between
binding/signature/casing/eqlog errors so the user actually sees them.

To match the existing eqlog-side conflict format the algebra path now
emits a single CompileError variant: ConflictingTermType. Drop the
recently-introduced ConflictingParentEl variant and lower every
TypeConflict -- both same-TypeId-with-differing-parents and outright
TypeId mismatch -- through one path. ConcreteTypes render as
`TypeName` for global types or `parent_name.TypeName` for member
types, with `parent_name` looked up in the structure's var_els (or
`?` for ambient els not bound to a variable).

Two adjustments line the algebra-side output up with the existing
eqlog-side diagnostics:

  - RuleStructures::semantic_els is now per-structure: each entry
    holds only terms whose lexical position lies in the scope walked
    into that structure. clone_structure used to deep-copy the
    parent's map forward, which made conflict anchoring pick up
    inherited terms (smaller TermIds, earlier in iteration order) at
    locations unrelated to the conflict. find_term now does a BFS
    over (StructureId, ElId) pairs starting at the conflict's
    structure: at each pair it scans semantic_els[s] for a class
    hit, and on miss expands through incoming morphisms by mapping
    back to preimages in each source structure. Cross-structure el
    dedup for Term::Var still goes through Structure::var_els (which
    is cloned with the rest of Structure), and for Term::App through
    Structure::func_apps.

  - Structure::close's typing pass now imposes argument types across
    every func/pred app before it imposes any codomain type. emit_app
    no longer eagerly writes the codomain into els at allocation
    time -- it leaves that to typing -- so the existing/new ordering
    on a conflicting el matches the order the eqlog-side typing rules
    use, and the `Could be ...` lines come out in the same sequence.
